[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":82},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":-7,"_18":19,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25},"tipo","metodo","cubo",{"_11":80,"_13":27,"_28":81},"id","sarah","nome","Sarah's Intermediate","nomeLongo","1st layer, top face and centers","termo","niveis",[55],"introducao","<p>Sarah Strong&#39;s method: build a face with its corners matching each other, make the top face one color with <a href=\"/en/glossary#sledgehammer\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Two triggers: the sledgehammer is R' F R F' and the hedgeslammer, F R' F' R. On the Skewb, they make up almost every algorithm in Sarah's…\">sledgehammers</a> and hedgeslammers and, at the end, put the centers in place. There are only 10 cases, one card each.</p>\n","etapas",[32,33,34],"historias",[26],{"_13":27,"_28":29,"_30":31},"Skewb","endereco","/historia#skewb","resumo","The Skewb was invented by Tony Durham and released by Uwe Mèffert around 1982, first as the \"Pyraminx Cube\"; the name Skewb came from Douglas Hofstadter, in his column about the Rubik's Cube.",{"_11":73,"_13":74,"_37":75,"_39":76,"_41":77,"_43":-7,"_44":78},{"_11":47,"_13":48,"_37":49,"_39":50,"_41":-7,"_43":51,"_44":52},{"_11":35,"_13":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":42,"_43":-7,"_44":45},"centros","Centers","html","<p>With both faces done, only the 4 side centers are left. Put them in place with the same idea as the <a href=\"/en/glossary#trigger\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A short sequence that shows up in many algorithms (R U R' U', Sledge…). Memorizing the triggers makes long algorithms easier.\">triggers</a>, holding the Skewb with a finished face on the side.</p>\n","grupos",[],"pagina",{"_13":36,"_28":46},"treino","imagem","/imagens/diagramas/994328254fbd22e6.svg","/skewb/centros","face-de-cima","Top face","<p>With the 1st layer on the bottom, make the whole top face yellow. Look at how many top corners already have yellow on top and where the others point: each case has its own card.</p>\n",[53],"/skewb/treino?grupo=intermediate","/imagens/diagramas/3ee61b793fac91d8.svg",{"_11":14,"_13":14,"_54":55,"_56":57,"_58":59,"_60":61,"_28":62},"nivel","intermediario","casos",10,"conjunto",false,"ids",[63,64,65,66,67,68,69,70,71,72],"/skewb/intermediate","SKEWB-Sledge","SKEWB-Hedge","SKEWB-DoubleSledge","SKEWB-SledgeYSledge","SKEWB-SledgeYiSledge","SKEWB-HedgeYHedge","SKEWB-HedgeYiHedge","SKEWB-SledgeYHedgeYHedge","SKEWB-SledgeYHedgeYiHedge","SKEWB-SledgeYiHedgeYHedge","primeira-camada","1st layer","<p>The white face on the bottom, with the 4 bottom corners matching each other on the sides. The side centers don&#39;t matter yet. It&#39;s intuitive: one corner at a time, no algorithm.</p>\n",[],{"_13":74,"_28":79},"/imagens/diagramas/dbdab867235079a6.svg","/skewb/primeira-camada","skewb","/skewb/",{"_83":84,"_85":86},"cubos",[89,90,91,92,93,94,95],"idiomas",[87,88],"pt","en",{"_11":103,"_13":103},{"_11":102,"_13":102},{"_11":101,"_13":101},{"_11":100,"_13":100},{"_11":98,"_13":99},{"_11":96,"_13":97},{"_11":80,"_13":27},"pyraminx","Pyraminx","megaminx","Megaminx","5x5","4x4","2x2","3x3"]
